WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.220
Completion-style prompts and chat-style prompts are related but not identical.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.220
A completion prompt asks the model to continue or respond to a block of text.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.120
A chat prompt usually has roles such as system, user, and assistant.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.595
Many instruction-tuned models are trained with specific structures that tell the model

00:00:15.595 --> 00:00:16.755
where instructions begin,

00:00:16.755 --> 00:00:20.255
where user content appears, and where the assistant response should start.

00:00:20.255 --> 00:00:22.030
Chat templates encode those structures.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.155
They may include special tokens, role markers, separators, or stop conditions.

00:00:26.155 --> 00:00:28.180
Different model families can expect different formatting.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:32.640
If the wrong template is used, a capable model may produce weak, oddly structured,

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.205
or instruction-misaligned output.

00:00:34.205 --> 00:00:37.830
It may repeat role labels, ignore system guidance, continue the user's text,

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.385
or stop too early.

00:00:39.385 --> 00:00:41.320
Tokenizer compatibility is part of the same issue.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.550
The model was trained to interpret text through a specific tokenizer and set of special

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:46.175
tokens.

00:00:46.175 --> 00:00:49.120
Prompt formatting should match that expectation as closely as possible.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.815
A runtime or web UI may apply templates automatically,

00:00:51.815 --> 00:00:55.030
but users still need to know when formatting is part of the problem they are

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:55.790
troubleshooting.

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.680
Test prompts consistently before judging model quality.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.880
Use the same model, quantization, context length, sampling settings,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.970
and chat template when comparing behavior.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:07.955
If the output is poor, verify formatting before assuming the model is bad.

00:01:07.955 --> 00:01:08.795
In local inference,

00:01:08.795 --> 00:01:12.960
many quality problems are actually configuration problems wearing a convincing disguise.
